  "summary": "Traditional Javanese vernacular house characterized by its pyramid-shaped roof, central pillar, and hierarchical spatial organization.",
  "description": "Joglo features a distinctive splayed roof structure supported by a central pillar (soko guru) and wooden brackets reflecting Javanese social hierarchy and cosmological beliefs. Its design creates a column-free central space for important gatherings while maintaining connection to natural elements.",
  "culturalContext": "The joglo — Java's noble house form — centers on the saka guru four main columns and interlocking tumpang sari roof tiers, symbolizing the owner's rank while making an earthquake-resilient timber assembly. The pendopo pavilion (open-sided, tiered roof) hosts ceremonies; the dalem inner rooms encode Javanese cosmology (north-south sacred axis, mountains-to-sea orientation). Joglo carpentry (wet-rice economy's craft apex) remains the template for Javanese weddings, government halls and cultural revival architecture.",
